<article class="env-lemma" id="00ID">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00ID">Lemma <span data-tag="00ID">10.50.12</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a valuation ring with field of fractions $K$. Set $\Gamma = K^*/A^*$ (with group law written additively). For $\gamma , \gamma ' \in \Gamma $ define $\gamma \geq \gamma '$ if and only if $\gamma - \gamma '$ is in the image of $A - \{ 0\}  \to \Gamma $. Then $(\Gamma , \geq )$ is a totally ordered abelian group. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Omitted, but follows easily from Lemma <a href="/tag/00IB" data-tag="00IB">10.50.4</a>. Note that in case $A = K$ we obtain the zero group $\Gamma = \{ 0\} $ endowed with its unique total ordering. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>